  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 239 AM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nCurrent nighttime microphysics RGB satellite product shows low \nstratus clouds in Nebraska trekking southwest. Guidance does keep\nthe stratus deck from entering Colorado (besides possibly the far\nnortheastern plains) this morning, which will help build a \nfavorable set-up for today's thunderstorm potential. Isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms are possible this afternoon, with \nconvection initiating in the mountains by late morning/early \nafternoon before spilling onto the adjacent plains through the \nearly evening. Modeled Skew-T soundings show steep low and mid- \nlevel lapse rates, with DCAPE values of 1300-1600 J/kg. This would\nfavor strong-to-severe outflow winds up to 60 mph, particularly \nfor the southern I-25 Corridor and Palmer Divide area where \ninstability will be best. SPC has a Marginal Risk (1/5) for severe\nstorms for that area today. However, any strong storm that \ndevelops over the plains has the potential for strong outflow \nwinds as well. While confidence is low, there is also an isolated \nhail threat with the strongest storms today. One hindrance to \ntoday's storms would be near-surface smoke from wildfires in the \nnorthwestern United States. If smoke is able to advect into the \nplains this afternoon, it could act as a cap for storm \ndevelopment. \n\nThe upper level ridge will likely restrengthen over the Four Corners \nregion by this weekend, which will bring the return of drier and \nwarmer conditions. Ensemble guidance continues to indicate \ntemperatures reaching near or over 100 F for the Urban Corridor by \nSunday. With Heat Risk expecting to get up to 3 (correlating to High \nRisk for much of the population, especially those sensitive and \nwithout effective cooling) have opted in to issue a Heat Advisory \nfor much of the Urban Corridor and the adjacent northern plains \n(including Greeley where temperatures could reach up to 105 F). \nMonday will likely see similar temperatures and an additional \nHeat Advisory may be needed in the next forecast package. \n\nWe will likely see a reprieve of hot temperatures by Tuesday, as an \nupper level trough is progged to trek north of Colorado. Ensemble \nsolutions favor temperatures in the mid-to-high 80s for much of the \nplains. We could also see scattered showers and thunderstorms by \nmidweek, however ensemble guidance does not seem too optimistic \nabout widespread measurable rainfall. Temperatures should climb\nback to the mid 90s towards the end of the week.",
